# Compose `{{ title }}` — write `src/scene.js`

The environment (`src/env.js`), zones (`src/zones/*.js`) and assets (`src/assets/*.js`,
`public/assets/*.glb`) exist. Write the entry module that wires them together.
(The harness normally generates this file itself; you are writing the fallback, so match the
generated shape exactly.)

## Contract
{{ frame_doc }}
{{ contract }}
`src/scene.js` must `export async function createScene({ THREE, renderer, loaders }) → { scene, cameras, update }`:
- `scene`: a `THREE.Scene`; build `ctx = { THREE, scene, renderer, loaders, heightAt, assets: {} }`,
  call `const env = (await buildEnv(ctx)) || {}` from `./env.js` (ONE argument: ctx), set
  `ctx.env = env`, then add every zone group — `build(ctx)` imported from `./zones/<snake>.js`
  (the export is named `build`, one argument) — naming each group after its zone.
- `cameras`: an array of PLAIN OBJECTS `{ name, position: [x,y,z], lookAt: [x,y,z], fov }` (the
  harness builds the PerspectiveCameras). Use the planned numbers literally. `cameras[0]` is the
  establishing shot: it looks at the SUBJECT point, not at the bounds centre, and its height is
  above anything standing between it and the subject.
- `update(t, dt)`: calls `env.update(t, dt)` and every zone group's `userData.update(t, dt)`
  (collect the list once at creation; never traverse per frame). `tickEnv` and `userData.tick`
  are not part of the contract — do not call them.
- Wrap each zone build in try/catch: a failing zone logs `console.error('[zone <name>] ' + err)` and is skipped.
- Use a seeded mulberry32 `rand` (seed 1) so frames are reproducible.
- GLB assets load via `loaders.gltf.loadAsync('/assets/<snake>.glb')` into `ctx.assets` before the
  zones build.

